# Bulwark -- redaction needles (TEMPLATE; safe to commit)
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   Fingerprints of our own infrastructure that must never leak out of a machine
#   through a user-submitted diagnostic bundle: the intel endpoint host, its IP
#   addresses, and the reputation-proxy bearer token. They do NOT appear in
#   appsettings.json (they come from fallback addresses baked into the binary and
#   from DNS-resolution lines in service.log), so the config-driven redaction in
#   bulwark.ps1 cannot derive them -- they have to be listed somewhere.
#
# WHY THIS TEMPLATE EXISTS
#   The real list (packaging\redaction-needles.txt) is plaintext, so it is excluded
#   both from every release package and from version control (see .gitignore). That
#   is correct -- but it meant the *format documentation* lived inside the excluded
#   file, i.e. the only people who ever needed it (fresh clone, CI runner, anyone
#   self-building) were exactly the people who never received it. verify_portable.ps1
#   used to fail with a bare "needle list missing" and one hard-coded path, which
#   reads like a broken script rather than "supply this out-of-band".
#
# WHERE THE REAL LIST GOES (verify_portable.ps1 resolves in this order)
#   1) $env:BULWARK_REDACTION_NEEDLES               -- CI secret / build machine
#   2) packaging\redaction-needles.txt              -- canonical, git-ignored
#   3) packaging\redaction-needles.local.txt        -- extra developer copy
#   4) %ProgramData%\Bulwark\redaction-needles.txt  -- release machine
#
# HOW IT IS CONSUMED
#   verify_portable.ps1     reads it as the sensitive-string scan pattern list
#   tools\test-redaction.ps1 same
#   bulwark.ps1             carries only SHA-256 of each entry (see
#                           $script:RedactNeedleHashes) and matches by hash, so the
#                           shipped log collector never contains the plaintext
#
# AFTER EDITING THE REAL LIST
#   Run:  powershell -File verify_portable.ps1 -Package <any package>
#   The "redaction needle set" section fails on desync and prints the exact hash
#   lines to paste into packaging\portable-scripts\bulwark.ps1.
#
# FORMAT
#   One needle per line. Blank lines and #-comments ignored. Matching is
#   case-insensitive (both sides are lowercased before hashing).
#
# ASCII-only: PS 5.1 on a zh-CN box reads files without a BOM as GBK.
#
# ---- EXAMPLE ENTRIES (replace with your real values) ----
# intel.example.invalid
# 203.0.113.10
# 198.51.100.7
# REPLACE_WITH_REAL_PROXY_TOKEN
